            "Most food poisoning in Ghana is not exotic; it is cooked food left in the <strong>danger zone</strong> between 5 and 63 °C, where bacteria double every 20 minutes, and food handled by someone who did not wash. The WHO <strong>five keys to safer food</strong> organise the teaching: <strong>keep clean</strong> (hands, surfaces, utensils, no flies or pests); <strong>separate raw and cooked</strong> (separate boards and knives, raw meat below cooked food, never the same bowl); <strong>cook thoroughly</strong> (75 °C in the core, juices clear, soups and stews boiling, reheat until piping hot once only); <strong>keep food at safe temperatures</strong> (do not leave cooked food out for more than two hours, refrigerate promptly, do not thaw at room temperature, keep hot food above 63 °C); and <strong>use safe water and raw materials</strong> (treated water, fresh ingredients, no food past its date, no bulging tins, washed fruit and vegetables). Specific Ghanaian hazards: <strong>aflatoxin</strong> in mouldy groundnuts and maize, which causes liver cancer and contributes to stunting — sort and discard the discoloured grains, dry and store properly; pesticide residues on vegetables sprayed close to harvest; the salad rinsed in a reused bowl; the fish smoked over tyres; and the drinking sachet handled by hands that handled money.",
