            "<strong>Occupational health</strong> extends the same thinking to the workplace, which in Ghana is mostly informal. Hazards are <strong>physical</strong> (noise, heat, vibration, radiation, machinery), <strong>chemical</strong> (pesticides, mercury, solvents, dust), <strong>biological</strong> (blood, tuberculosis, zoonoses, snakes), <strong>ergonomic</strong> (lifting, head-loading, posture) and <strong>psychosocial</strong> (shift work, violence, stress). The <strong>farmer</strong> sprays organophosphates without protection, decants them into drink bottles, reuses the container for water and presents with the salivation, sweating, small pupils and twitching of poisoning; he is also bitten by snakes and worn down by the hoe. The <strong>small-scale miner</strong> breathes silica, handles mercury with bare hands and heats the amalgam indoors, and dies in collapsing pits and flooded shafts. The <strong>market woman</strong> and the <strong>food seller</strong> have back pain and burns; the <strong>head porter</strong> (kayayoo) neck and spine injuries and the risks of sleeping on the street; the <strong>driver</strong> fatigue and the road; the <strong>e-waste burner</strong> at the scrap yard lead, cadmium and dioxins; the <strong>fisherman</strong> drowning and the <strong>fish smoker</strong> smoke; the <strong>health worker</strong> sharps, tuberculosis, violence and back injury. Prevention follows the <strong>hierarchy of controls</strong>: eliminate the hazard, substitute a safer one, engineer it out (guards, ventilation, a retort for mercury), control it administratively (training, rotation, rest, pre-placement and periodic examinations) and, last and least reliable, personal protective equipment. The Labour Act, 2003 (Act 651), the Factories, Offices and Shops Act, 1970 (Act 328) and the Workmen's Compensation Law, 1987 (PNDCL 187) set the duties and the compensation; the nurse in an occupational setting runs the first-aid post, screens and surveys injuries and illness, educates, and reports the hazards to those who can remove them.",
